1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,495
Picture Sam the afternoon before Harlan's first real batch, staring at a blank

2
00:00:04,495 --> 00:00:05,185
prompt sheet.

3
00:00:05,185 --> 00:00:09,649
He could start typing angles straight away, or spend twenty minutes finding out what

4
00:00:09,649 --> 00:00:11,934
these tools are actually reliable at first.

5
00:00:11,934 --> 00:00:16,291
He chose the twenty minutes, and it's the reason the batch you'll see next lecture

6
00:00:16,291 --> 00:00:18,576
only has two problems in it instead of six.

7
00:00:18,576 --> 00:00:22,508
Quick warning before the assessment: I'm going to name real tools and real

8
00:00:22,508 --> 00:00:26,440
capabilities, and some of those specifics will drift within months of this

9
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:26,971
recording.

10
00:00:26,971 --> 00:00:28,724
That's not a flaw in the lecture.

11
00:00:28,724 --> 00:00:32,922
It's the reason the rest of this course is built around a brief and a test, not

12
00:00:32,922 --> 00:00:33,985
around any one tool.

13
00:00:33,985 --> 00:00:38,183
Here's where things actually stand as I'm recording this, checked against real,

14
00:00:38,183 --> 00:00:42,115
current results rather than recalled from memory or last year's headlines.

15
00:00:42,665 --> 00:00:47,273
Here's the honest split, and it's more nuanced than "AI images are good now" or "AI

16
00:00:47,273 --> 00:00:49,882
images can't be trusted." Which is it actually?

17
00:00:49,882 --> 00:00:53,491
Both, depending entirely on which specific claim you're checking.

18
00:00:53,491 --> 00:00:57,155
Photorealistic scenes are strong across most of the leading tools.

19
00:00:57,155 --> 00:01:01,763
Short text, a tagline, a single word, a number, has genuinely improved to the point

20
00:01:01,763 --> 00:01:05,927
of being usable directly, which wasn't true even a couple of years earlier.

21
00:01:05,927 --> 00:01:10,313
But long text, and this matters a lot for a spec-driven brand like Harlan's, is

22
00:01:10,313 --> 00:01:14,921
still weak: every additional line of text is another chance for the model to drop a

23
00:01:14,921 --> 00:01:16,531
word or misplace a character.

24
00:01:16,531 --> 00:01:21,028
And getting an exact logo or brand typeface right, or getting the same brand feel

25
00:01:21,028 --> 00:01:25,525
twice in a row without curating it yourself, is still not something you can trust

26
00:01:25,525 --> 00:01:26,136
unattended.

27
00:01:26,686 --> 00:01:31,409
There's one finding worth being precise about, because it changes how you actually

28
00:01:31,409 --> 00:01:34,116
work: text rendering hasn't uniformly improved.

29
00:01:34,116 --> 00:01:35,441
It's improved unevenly.

30
00:01:35,441 --> 00:01:40,106
A single word or a short tagline is close to solved territory now, across several

31
00:01:40,106 --> 00:01:40,912
serious tools.

32
00:01:40,912 --> 00:01:45,578
A full sentence, a paragraph, or a busy layout with several text regions is still

33
00:01:45,578 --> 00:01:49,897
genuinely hard, because each region the model has to render correctly is an

34
00:01:49,897 --> 00:01:52,316
independent chance to get something wrong.

35
00:01:52,316 --> 00:01:56,809
Think of it as compounding odds: get one word right ninety-nine times out of a

36
00:01:56,809 --> 00:02:01,647
hundred, and a five-word line still fails noticeably more often than a one-word line

37
00:02:01,647 --> 00:02:01,935
does.

38
00:02:01,935 --> 00:02:06,715
The practical answer, and you'll build this properly in Section 3.4, is to generate

39
00:02:06,715 --> 00:02:11,438
the image clean and add exact copy afterward for anything longer than a few words.

40
00:02:11,438 --> 00:02:15,873
That workaround stays true no matter which tool is winning the benchmark this

41
00:02:15,873 --> 00:02:20,135
quarter, because it doesn't depend on the generator's text feature at all.

42
00:02:20,685 --> 00:02:23,375
Here's the practical rule to actually work by.

43
00:02:23,375 --> 00:02:28,112
Trust the clean scene and the short tagline by default, spot-checking rather than

44
00:02:28,112 --> 00:02:29,574
scrutinising every pixel.

45
00:02:29,574 --> 00:02:34,428
Verify, every single time without exception, anything that's an exact brand colour,

46
00:02:34,428 --> 00:02:39,106
an exact logo, a spec number rendered as text, or consistency across more than a

47
00:02:39,106 --> 00:02:40,861
couple of images in one batch.

48
00:02:40,861 --> 00:02:45,481
You'll see exactly why that verification step matters in the next few lectures,

49
00:02:45,481 --> 00:02:49,516
because Harlan's first real batch fails on two of those exact points.

50
00:02:49,516 --> 00:02:54,311
Most marketers who've tried batch AI image generation report the same frustration:

51
00:02:54,311 --> 00:02:58,931
real adoption, but real difficulty turning it into work they can actually ship.

52
00:02:58,931 --> 00:03:03,785
That gap almost always traces back to skipping this exact verification step, not to

53
00:03:03,785 --> 00:03:05,949
the generation itself being unusable.

54
00:03:06,499 --> 00:03:10,454
One more honest limit, and it applies to me as much as it applies to you.

55
00:03:10,454 --> 00:03:14,734
Whatever specific tools get named in the next few lectures were checked against

56
00:03:14,734 --> 00:03:19,177
their actual current capabilities at the time of writing, not recalled from memory

57
00:03:19,177 --> 00:03:19,773
or assumed.

58
00:03:19,773 --> 00:03:22,049
Do the same before you brief a real batch.

59
00:03:22,049 --> 00:03:26,492
Five minutes confirming a tool still does what you think it does is cheaper than a

60
00:03:26,492 --> 00:03:29,146
batch built on a capability that quietly changed.

61
00:03:29,146 --> 00:03:31,097
This isn't a one-time check, either.

62
00:03:31,097 --> 00:03:35,594
Build it into a habit: before any batch that matters, spend five minutes confirming

63
00:03:35,594 --> 00:03:39,929
the specific claim you're relying on, whether that's a text-rendering feature, a

64
00:03:39,929 --> 00:03:42,800
consistency tool, or anything else this course names.

65
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:46,322
Tools in this category move quarter to quarter, not year to year.

66
00:03:46,872 --> 00:03:49,652
Next lecture is where generation actually starts.

67
00:03:49,652 --> 00:03:54,248
You'll see Harlan's real first batch, including the two things that go wrong with

68
00:03:54,248 --> 00:03:58,843
it, because a batch that works perfectly on the first try would be a worse lesson

69
00:03:58,843 --> 00:04:00,715
than the one you're about to see.

70
00:04:00,715 --> 00:04:05,198
Before you sit down to generate anything of your own, write down those same two

71
00:04:05,198 --> 00:04:09,680
checks: your exact brand colour, stated precisely, and any spec text a customer

72
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:11,268
needs to read exactly right.

73
00:04:11,268 --> 00:04:15,410
Five minutes, and it's the same list Section 3.6 turns into artifact B03.
